# Configuration. Copy to .env and edit, or export these directly. Nothing here
# is secret except the API keys at the bottom, but all of it is specific to one
# machine, which is why .env is ignored and this file is not.
#
# The engine block below can also live in an instance's own .env, so two
# instances can run against two different models. The keys cannot: see the
# credentials block. references/instance.md is the contract.

# copy | postiz | command      (copy is the default and needs nothing else)
LINKEDIN_PUBLISH=copy

# --- postiz -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The channel id. Check it against your integration list before you use it:
# a personal profile and a company page are two lines here and two very
# different things in a feed.
POSTIZ_INTEGRATION_ID=
# The human name of that channel. Optional, and worth setting: it is what the
# publish plan prints back at you before anything is sent.
POSTIZ_INTEGRATION_NAME=

# --- command ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Your own program. The post arrives on its stdin.
LINKEDIN_PUBLISH_CMD=

# --- engine, the local app -------------------------------------------------
# Also readable from <instance>/.env. The process environment wins over it.

# anthropic | openai      (openai means any endpoint speaking that chat format,
#                          so OpenAI, Mistral, OpenRouter, Ollama, LM Studio)
#
# Local and hosted are the same code path here, and neither is the recommended
# one. What decides is what the model can do, and there are three things:
# hold a system block of about 6400 tokens without its context window cutting
# it, answer a forced tool call, and produce the five field validation sheet
# when asked instead of carrying on interviewing. A small local model can miss
# the last two, the app says so on screen when it happens, and docs/smoke.md
# carries the measurements.
VERBATIM_PROVIDER=anthropic
# The model id, exactly as the provider spells it.
VERBATIM_MODEL=
# The endpoint, when it is not the provider's own. A local runtime looks like
# http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1 for Ollama, http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1 for LM Studio.
# For OpenRouter it is https://openrouter.ai/api/v1, and the model id carries
# the vendor, as in anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.
# Ollama picks its context window from free VRAM and defaults to 4096, which
# is under what this app sends: raise it with OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH on the
# server, or the skill is truncated and the model answers anyway.
# Set here, an instance .env can only name the provider's own endpoint or a
# runtime on this machine: a file that travels does not get to decide where a
# key is sent. For a hosted third party, either export this variable yourself
# or name its host in VERBATIM_ENDPOINT_OK below.
VERBATIM_BASE_URL=

# --- credentials, process environment only ---------------------------------
# Never write these in an instance directory. An instance holds a person's
# material; it gets copied, synced and sometimes committed. The app refuses to
# start when it finds a key shaped name in an instance .env.
# VERBATIM_API_KEY wins over the provider specific ones, and local inference
# usually needs none at all.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
VERBATIM_API_KEY=

# Hosts an instance .env is allowed to send the key to, comma separated. This
# lives next to the key on purpose: it is the pairing that is the decision.
# Example: openrouter.ai, api.mistral.ai
VERBATIM_ENDPOINT_OK=
